  "explanation": "The proposal investigates the cross-lingual transferability of uncertainty-based abstention thresholds from English to Urdu factual QA and proposes a post-hoc threshold re-calibration using a small validation set. While selective classification and confidence calibration are widely studied in English, and cross-lingual transfer has been explored for generation and alignment tasks, examining abstention threshold shifts under translation artifacts in low-resource settings like Urdu is moderately original. However, the core methodology relies heavily on standard threshold tuning and existing uncertainty metrics, positioning it as an incremental application rather than a foundational algorithmic contribution.",
  "problems": [
    "The proposed method relies directly on standard uncertainty metrics (verbalized probabilities and entropy) without introducing novel confidence estimation techniques tailored to translation artifacts.",
    "The reliance on translated evaluation sets may conflate translation noise with genuine cross-lingual calibration shifts.",
    "The scope is restricted to a single target language (Urdu), limiting the generalizability of the findings across typologically diverse low-resource languages."
  ],
  "improvements": [
    "Expand the evaluation to include multiple typologically diverse low-resource languages to demonstrate broader generalizability of the cross-lingual abstention gap.",
    "Introduce a specialized uncertainty score or a robust aggregation mechanism that explicitly accounts for translation uncertainty rather than relying solely on standard token entropy.",
    "Compare the post-hoc re-calibration baseline against existing domain-adaptation or few-shot threshold learning baselines."
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "passage_id": "s_dc2a3ff62aa412fbe9b5",
      "claim": "Quantifying model confidence and uncertainty is critical for trustworthy user-facing applications.",
      "relation": "context"
    },
    {
      "passage_id": "s_cfb58c36206edae5a926",
      "claim": "Capabilities of large language models remain largely limited to high-resource languages.",
      "relation": "context"
    },
    {
      "passage_id": "s_4fbefa1ca18e25b6bdca",
      "claim": "Cross-lingual transfer of evaluation and scoring mechanisms from English to other languages is an active area of study.",
      "relation": "overlaps"
    }
  ],
  "uncertainty": "The primary limitation is that translation artifacts from automated pipelines may artificially inflate entropy and skew uncertainty metrics independently of the underlying model's true factual reliability in Urdu.",
  "tradeoffs": [
    "Restricting the scope to Urdu fits the constrained compute resources (12GB GPU / API) but limits the broader claims regarding low-resource cross-lingual transferability."
  ],
  "falsification_test": "If an English-calibrated abstention threshold transferred to Urdu maintains zero-shot selective accuracy within 2% of the oracle Urdu-calibrated threshold across multiple datasets, the hypothesis that systematic calibration shift occurs due to language differences is falsified."
}
